# Member summary design candidate

## Problem

The summary currently renders `MemberDetails.summary.currencies` directly in `MemberSummary.svelte`. Its overview, counts and currency labels occupy the left chart column. `MemberIdentity` owns the name, metadata and tabs but receives no total. The API adapter already validates decimal strings and dates, and the funder/history consumers still need transport currency fields. Farage's real response has a GBP total of `2323720.45`; the employment labels span current IDs 1 and 2 and legacy ID 12. `registeredAt` is the only date available, so the weekly plot must describe registration timing.

## Usage

```svelte
<!-- EntityDetails member branch -->
{@const summary = summarizeMember(details)}
<MemberIdentity member={details.member} view={view} total={summary.total} />
{#if view === "history"}
  <MemberHistory {details} />
{:else}
  <MemberSummary {details} {summary} />
{/if}
```

`MemberIdentity` uses `formatPounds(total)` and a compact “Total declared value” label. `MemberSummary` renders `summary.categories` and `summary.earnings`, with no currency loops. The adapter returns exact values plus chart geometry; components do not classify categories, bucket dates or sum numbers.

## Shape

Prefer a local presentation module, `lib/member-summary.ts`, with one main operation. Preserve `MemberDetails` and `readMember` as the shared API contract.

```ts
export interface MemberSummary {
  total: string | null;
  categories: readonly CategoryValue[];
  earnings: EarningsSeries;
}
export interface CategoryValue {
  id: number;
  name: string;
  amount: string | null;
  incomplete: boolean;
  width: number;
  negative: boolean;
}
export interface EarningsWeek {
  start: string; // Monday UTC, YYYY-MM-DD
  amount: string | null;
  incomplete: boolean;
}
export type EarningsSeries =
  | { kind: "empty" }
  | { kind: "undated" }
  | { kind: "weeks"; weeks: readonly EarningsWeek[]; hasUndated: boolean };
export function summarizeMember(details: MemberDetails): MemberSummary;
export function formatPounds(amount: string | null): string;
```

Keep decimal arithmetic, employment recognition and Monday bucketing private in this module. Export only data that the view actually renders. Exact amounts remain strings; geometry may use bounded numbers derived from BigInt ratios. Do not convert amounts to Number before summation or formatting. An internal `{ coefficient: bigint, scale: number }` accumulator aligns arbitrary decimal scales, preserving the existing nine-decimal fixture. A fixed pence accumulator would silently violate the current transport contract.

The server GBP group remains authoritative for the top total and category amounts. Choose `currency === "GBP"` once inside `summarizeMember`. Do not add non-GBP groups together or treat their values as pounds. The outward model has no currency property. Format using the existing string grouping conventions with a pound prefix, normalize integral/trailing-zero amounts to at least two decimal places without discarding additional precision, and put minus before the pound sign.

Weekly earnings come from GBP entries in declarations classified as employment. Use the source category name as the semantic discriminator, with normalized whitespace/case and a narrow exact family: `Employment and earnings`, its ` - Ad hoc payments` and ` - Ongoing paid employment` variants, plus `Employment` if preserving the existing fixture spelling. Own these source aliases in exactly one private function, never numeric IDs alone. An unrelated category assigned ID 1 must not become earnings, and legacy ID 12 with the employment label must work. This avoids guessing the source schema from IDs; it does not claim to solve future unknown source vocabularies.

Each qualifying declaration belongs to the week containing its UTC registration instant. Clear UTC clock fields, then subtract `(getUTCDay() + 6) % 7` days. Aggregate all entries exactly; keep duplicates because they are stored funding records. Sort ascending and insert every missing Monday from the first to the last qualifying dated declaration. Missing weeks represent no recorded earnings and have exact zero; a week containing only unavailable amounts has null; a week with known and unavailable amounts has its known sum and `incomplete: true`. Negative amounts remain signed. Undated entries never enter a dated bucket. Unknown currency entries cannot supply a pound amount and should make a qualifying bucket incomplete if other GBP records establish it, without introducing currency copy in the UI.

Differentiate no earnings declarations from earnings declarations whose dates are all missing. Avoid inventing an earned date or current-week extrapolation. A single dated week renders one meaningful bar. Zero-only weeks render a baseline and explicit values rather than a zero-height empty SVG. Include dated unknown-only qualifying declarations in the period so an unavailable amount does not disappear from the time range.

## Layout and chart behavior

Put the member identity and total in a two-column header row, identity left and total right, tabs across the full width underneath. At narrow widths stack the total beneath metadata and above tabs. Place “Declared value by category” and “Earnings by registration week” side by side below the tabs. Categories use horizontal signed bars with real labels and exact amounts. The weekly chart uses bars, a visible zero baseline, signed positive/negative bounds, sparse UTC month/year ticks and exact values available to keyboard and pointer users. A nearby disclosure containing an accessible date/value table is a robust fallback for a long weekly series; the SVG itself needs a clear label and concise description. A tooltip reachable only by pointer is insufficient.

Use one short subtitle “Grouped by the week registered” if the heading is shortened to “Earnings per week”. No overview count/context block returns. An incomplete amount or missing date may need a concise chart-local state, not a replacement wall of coverage statistics. Retain recent declarations and the history link as existing navigation.

## Scope

Add `lib/member-summary.ts`, optionally `MemberEarningsChart.svelte` to keep SVG layout out of the summary template, and focused summary calculation tests. Change `EntityDetails.svelte`, `MemberIdentity.svelte`, `MemberSummary.svelte` and the relevant CSS. Remove obsolete `.member-currency-totals`, `.category-currency`, overview and coverage styling only after checking remaining callers. CSS currently defines `.member-total` twice and appends summary overrides at the bottom; consolidate these rules when changing the layout. Preserve history and funder behavior and their existing tests.

## Alternatives considered

A central API adapter could replace the member summary DTO with the presentation model inside `readMember`, then components would receive ready-to-render data without a derivation call. It hides arithmetic as well as this design but couples a shared transport boundary to page layout, complicates retention of the original currency summary, and makes route loading responsible for graph policy. It is viable if the entire member API model is intentionally redesigned, but larger than this task.

Embedding computations in `MemberSummary.svelte` minimizes file count but forces the header to compute its own total or forces callbacks/slots to push derived data upward. That splits ownership and makes weekly logic hard to exercise independently. Reject it.

Replacing API totals with a single frontend aggregation of all declarations would make reconciliation automatic, but loses the current server-authoritative total contract and breaks deliberately independent server-total tests. The local model should acknowledge the two authoritative inputs: server totals for aggregate values, full declarations for dates absent from summary.

## Verification and risks

Test the exact Farage top total `£2,323,720.45`; precision beyond Number's integer limit; Monday/Sunday boundaries and timezone offsets; a missing week; December/January; one week; all-zero and negative series; known-plus-null and null-only values; undated-only and empty declarations; duplicate entries; legacy employment ID 12 and employment labels under unfamiliar IDs; unrelated category ID 1; other currencies never relabeled pounds. Existing fixture category totals intentionally disagree with declaration records, so do not make an accidental consistency assertion against that fixture.

Check desktop header alignment, both charts and the real record at 1440px, plus 360px overflow and keyboard chart access. The week is a registration week, not a claim about when earnings occurred. No API or Rust changes are required.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate recommendation only; parent arena selects the base and grafts.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation performed.

## Next implementation step

Implement the pure summary projection and a fixture exercising exact weekly aggregation, then connect the same projection to the header and both charts.
# Member summary model candidate

## Problem

The member screen currently gives its overview a full column, repeats currency sections, and displays declaration and funding counts. `EntityDetails.svelte` loads parsed `MemberDetails`, renders identity, and chooses summary or history. `MemberSummary.svelte` renders API summary totals and categories. History and funder screens share `entity-details.ts`, so changing its transport contracts or `formatAmount` would expand this work. The new summary needs one GBP total in the identity header, a category graph, and a weekly earnings graph. The API supplies registration dates, so the weekly graph must say "Earnings registered per week" and "Week registered" rather than claim when payments occurred.

## Usage

`EntityDetails.svelte` derives one view model after a member response. It passes the total to the identity and the graphs to the summary. Svelte components only render their inputs.

```ts
import { memberSummary, type MemberSummaryModel } from "./lib/member-summary";

// Within the loaded member branch, or a derived value scoped to it.
const summary = memberSummary(details);
// <MemberIdentity member={details.member} view={view} total={summary.total} />
// <MemberSummary memberId={details.member.id} summary={summary} />
```

Render the header total only on the summary route, beside identity content and before the tabs, as clarified by the parent. The summary graph components receive no raw entries. Existing recent declarations can remain below the graphs, if retained, with the action "View declaration history" and no declaration count. The totals and graphs do not need prose context blocks.

## Shape

Add `frontend/src/lib/member-summary.ts`. It owns GBP selection, exact decimal arithmetic, category presentation, earnings category membership, registration week bucketing, missing amount states, and graph scaling. Keep its decimal representation and helper functions private. Export one builder and its returned model types.

```ts
export type SummaryValue =
  | { kind: "known"; text: string }
  | { kind: "partial"; text: string }
  | { kind: "unavailable"; text: "Amount unavailable" }
  | { kind: "empty"; text: "No GBP amounts" };

export interface CategoryPoint {
  id: number;
  label: string;
  value: SummaryValue;
  // Coordinates in a shared signed scale, always finite and bounded.
  from: number;
  to: number;
}
export interface RegisteredWeek {
  start: string; // Monday YYYY-MM-DD in UTC
  label: string;
  value: SummaryValue;
  from: number;
  to: number;
}
export interface MemberSummaryModel {
  total: SummaryValue;
  categories: readonly CategoryPoint[];
  categoryZero: number;
  earnings: {
    kind: "ready" | "empty" | "unavailable";
    weeks: readonly RegisteredWeek[];
    zero: number;
    hasUndatedAmounts: boolean;
  };
}
export function memberSummary(details: MemberDetails): MemberSummaryModel {
  // not implemented
}
```

`SummaryValue` preserves the distinction between zero, partial knowledge, unavailable amounts, and no GBP data. It does not export counts for the UI. A partial value can expose a short accessible "Known amount" label without restoring the removed coverage blocks. For unknown-only weeks, the SVG uses an unavailable marker rather than a zero bar. A missing week between dated earnings declarations represents zero registered earnings, not an amount inferred for an undated declaration.

The builder uses the server's GBP summary amount and categories for the total/category graph. This maintains existing authoritative aggregate behavior. It builds weekly earnings from declarations whose category IDs are 1, 2, or 12 and entries whose currency equals GBP. That finite earnings rule exists in one private predicate. It never matches human category names. The API remains the source of category names and IDs.

Private decimal arithmetic uses a signed `bigint` coefficient with a decimal scale. Addition aligns scales before summing, retains all decimal digits, and canonicalizes signed zero. Formatting adds £ without converting amounts to `number`. Only bounded normalized graph coordinates become numbers. Currency presentation is owned here, so funder pages retain their current format. `MemberDeclarations.svelte` also displays GBP entries with £, so recent declarations and history have consistent GBP presentation. Keep that formatting independent of the summary aggregation builder.

Registration timestamps are already validated by the transport parser. Convert each date to a UTC Monday at midnight. Weeks run continuously from the first dated qualifying GBP earnings declaration to the last, with zero weeks in gaps. Null dates never receive an invented week. Known undated values stay in the header and category totals but cannot enter the weekly series. Null amounts contribute no money and mark their bucket unavailable or partial. Negative entries remain negative. Known zero dates produce a real zero point and establish the range. There is no use of the global summary registration range because gifts could stretch an earnings graph beyond its earnings dates.

This is a deep module with one operation. It hides exact arithmetic and time rules from rendering while returning the values required for header and graphs. It avoids a mutable store, transport changes, and a sequence of helpers that components must coordinate.

## Layout and file scope

`MemberIdentity.svelte` accepts the derived total. Use a header grid with member title and metadata on the left, an end-aligned total on the right, and tabs spanning the width. The total label is "Declared value" and the value is £2,323,720.45 for member 5091. On narrow widths it moves below identity content.

`MemberSummary.svelte` replaces its overview column and repeated currencies with two sections. The category section uses a horizontal graph because its category labels are long. The weekly section uses a compact SVG with a visible zero baseline, signed bars, readable sparse date ticks, and per-week accessible labels. A table or disclosure listing exact weekly amounts gives keyboard users access to every point. The two graphs can occupy equal desktop columns, with categories allowed enough width for wrapped labels. Stack them below 800px. Existing CSS already defines the 800px summary breakpoint and signed category bars; revise those member selectors and remove orphaned overview selectors. Funder selectors stay unchanged.

Files changed: `MemberDeclarations.svelte` for GBP amount presentation, the new model module, `EntityDetails.svelte`, `MemberIdentity.svelte`, `MemberSummary.svelte`, member rules in `styles.css`, and `tests/entity-details.spec.ts`. Add model tests only if the project's existing test runner can directly exercise TS modules without a new testing dependency; otherwise cover boundary cases through mocked responses in the existing Playwright suite.

## Alternatives considered

Component local calculations would place currency policy in the header and weekly policy in the summary, with chart math split across components. The proposed builder gives both components the same derived model through one call.

A changed API response with weekly aggregates would centralize earnings policy server side, but requires Rust domain and serialization changes and expands scope beyond this frontend request. It also changes the response for consumers that still need all currencies.

A Svelte store for member aggregates would introduce lifetime and reactive ownership despite the loaded detail response already being immutable for the page. A pure builder needs no subscriptions or synchronization.

## Tradeoffs accepted

- We accept the existing API summary as the source for total/category amounts and raw declarations as the source for weeks. The real fixture proves those representations have different coverage, so tests must not require their grand totals to match.
- We accept category IDs 1, 2, and 12 as a single documented frontend earnings classifier until the API exposes that domain meaning.
- We accept explicit missing states in the model without user-facing count blocks. A value of zero must not disguise unknown data.

## Verification evidence and plan

Read `MemberSummary.svelte`, `MemberIdentity.svelte`, `EntityDetails.svelte`, `entity-details.ts`, member CSS, and relevant Playwright assertions. Current tests at `entity-details.spec.ts:733` explicitly expect three currency totals, a signed negative category, and a currency coverage note; replace these summary assertions while preserving history and funder behavior tests. Existing amounts exceed JavaScript safe integer precision and have nine decimal places, so that fixture remains valuable for exact formatting.

Read `/tmp/exposed-member-5091.json` with Python Decimal. Its API summary gives GBP 2323720.45. Categories 1 and 2 sum to 2057097.84; declaration categories include 12 even though its GBP summary categories do not. Weekly classification must include category 12 without assuming every such declaration has a known amount. These observations ground the design; no implementation was executed.

Verify exact sums with 0.1 plus 0.2, large amounts and arbitrary scales; dated negative and zero values; Sunday/Monday and timezone offset transitions; continuous gap weeks; partial and unknown-only buckets; all-undated earnings; empty and non-GBP-only summaries; category 12; and gift exclusion from weeks. Run existing frontend check, build, and relevant Playwright tests. Drive real member 5091 and capture desktop screenshots from the implementation worktree. If publishing a PR, upload those as GitHub attachments and inspect the rendered description as required by AGENTS.md.

## Synthesis decision

Candidate only. The parent architect chooses the base and records grafts after comparing candidates.

## Implementation reconciliation

No implementation performed. All repository inspection was read only.

## Next implementation step

Implement the pure member summary builder and its edge-case verification before changing Svelte markup.
# Member summary redesign candidate

## Grounded model

`EntityDetails.svelte` owns data loading and chooses `MemberIdentity` plus either `MemberSummary` or `MemberHistory`. The identity component sees only the `MemberProfile`; the summary owns the loaded `MemberDetails` and currently renders server totals and category bars. `entity-details.ts` parses API data to typed `MemberDetails`; `summary.currencies[]` contains exact decimal-string totals grouped by currency/category, while `declarations[]` contains category and nullable `registeredAt` plus each entry's exact decimal-string amount and nullable currency. Dates are validated as parseable strings, not normalized to dates. `formatAmount` formats exact decimal text without currency. Existing category bars use BigInt scaling, preserve sign and avoid `Number` money arithmetic.

No chart dependency or chart component pattern appears in these files. Existing CSS draws category bars, but the summary spec already asserts these class structures and labels. The history UI filters declaration registration dates, so registration-time semantics are established in the UI. The profile header belongs to `MemberIdentity`, so placing a total at its upper right needs either a prop or a wrapper/layout change; adding it in `MemberSummary` would visually put it below the header and fail the request.

## Design

Use one small pure view-model adapter in `entity-details.ts` to produce chart-ready values, keeping API parsing and all currency amounts as exact decimal strings. Give `MemberIdentity` the GBP `Total` (or a narrow `{amount, unknownAmountCount}` value), render that in a right-aligned header block. GBP formats as `£` plus the existing exact grouping; all other currencies retain their currency code. If no GBP total exists, show an unavailable/empty state, never a fabricated £0. Existing server summaries remain the source for lifetime and category totals.

`MemberSummary` becomes a vertical summary: lifetime known value stays at the top of the summary content only if not promoted to the header; then category chart and weekly earnings chart; then recent declarations. Remove entry-count/context prose blocks (stored declaration count, entry counts, category intro). Keep data-quality notes needed to interpret partial or missing amounts. Currency grouping is retained: never combine different currencies into one monetary sum. For the supplied Nigel Farage record, all 124 entries are GBP, so the displayed total is `£2,323,720.45`.

Prefer a focused SVG chart component over stretching CSS bars into a time-series chart. SVG supports a baseline, labels, varying weekly columns, negative bars, and a compact accessible description without adding a chart library. Keep chart input/output types and exact arithmetic in the domain adapter; SVG rendering stays presentational. Category chart may retain the existing horizontal proportional-bar presentation but format GBP amounts with £. Weekly graph aggregates only earnings category IDs 1 (ad hoc), 2 (ongoing), and 12 (legacy employment earnings); label it **Earnings by registration week**. The dates are `registeredAt`, not payment/earned dates. State this in the accessible title/caption and show a short visible sublabel such as “Grouped by declaration registration week.”

Construct Monday UTC buckets from `registeredAt`: parse the ISO instant, take its UTC calendar date, then its Monday. Fill every Monday from the first to last dated earning entry so quiet weeks have a zero baseline. Preserve negative aggregate values below the baseline. Skip undated declarations and report the count; if there are no dated GBP earnings, render a clear empty message rather than an empty graph. Where any earnings amount/currency is unknown, plot the known subtotal and state that the weekly totals are partial; don't coerce unknown values to zero silently. Avoid JS `Number` for money; use a common decimal scale and BigInt for sum and bar scaling, converting only the final normalized geometry to a finite SVG coordinate.

## Example data check

From `/tmp/exposed-member-5091.json`:

- Known GBP lifetime amount: £2,323,720.45 (124 GBP entries).
- Category 1, “Employment and earnings - Ad hoc payments”: £1,951,169.44 (101 entries).
- Category 2, ongoing employment and earnings: £105,928.40 (3 entries).
- Category 12 has no GBP entries in this snapshot. Earnings categories 1/2/12 therefore total £2,057,097.84.
- Earnings series has 30 nonzero registered weeks across 2024-07-29 to 2026-09-14, from 104 entries; no earnings entries are undated. Fill intervening Mondays with zero. (All-declaration registration range is 2024-07-22 to 2026-09-17.)

## Suggested module surface

In `frontend/src/lib/entity-details.ts`:

```ts
export interface WeeklyAmount {
  week: string; // YYYY-MM-DD, Monday in UTC
  amount: string; // exact signed decimal; zero buckets included
  unknownAmountCount: number;
}

export interface EarningsSeries {
  currency: string;
  weeks: WeeklyAmount[];
  undatedEntryCount: number;
  unknownAmountCount: number;
  unknownCurrencyCount: number;
}

export function earningsByRegistrationWeek(
  declarations: MemberDeclaration[],
  currency: string,
): EarningsSeries;

export function formatCurrencyAmount(amount: string | null, currency: string): string;
```

An implementation may use a canonical `EARNING_CATEGORY_IDS` constant (`1, 2, 12`) next to the helper to avoid duplicating the vocabulary in component markup. Convert each external `registeredAt` to a validated UTC Monday at the aggregation boundary. If `week` remains a string for `Intl`, keep it canonical and parse it only in the chart formatter. Keep `currency` in API types and all funder consumers untouched.

A component API can remain small:

```svelte
<!-- MemberIdentity.svelte -->
<MemberIdentity {member} {view} totals={details.summary.currencies} />

<!-- EarningsByWeek.svelte -->
<EarningsByWeek series={earningsByRegistrationWeek(...)} />
```

`EarningsByWeek` receives already aggregated exact values and renders inline `<svg role="img" aria-labelledby=...>` with a title and description; do not make it own domain filtering or currency conversion. A no-data result uses a textual `<p>` instead. Use accessible date tick labels and a currency-aware y-axis/tooltip or a list/table equivalent for exact value access.

## File scope and UX risks

- `frontend/src/MemberIdentity.svelte`: accept the summary currency totals and align the known total at the upper right beside the name; wrap responsively.
- `frontend/src/MemberSummary.svelte`: remove count/context blocks, format GBP with £, retain unknown-data notes, add earnings series component.
- `frontend/src/EarningsByWeek.svelte` (new): accessible SVG and empty state only.
- `frontend/src/lib/entity-details.ts`: exact weekly aggregation, currency formatter, canonical earnings category IDs; no change to transport fields/API or funder presentation.
- `frontend/src/styles.css`: header alignment/wrap and SVG axis, baseline, positive/negative bars, sparse month ticks; remove obsolete summary layout styles.
- `frontend/tests/entity-details.spec.ts`: adjust display expectations and add behavior coverage for Monday UTC grouping, skipped-week zeros, negative values, unknown amount/currency, and undated entries. Verify category summary uses server amounts and GBP prefix.

Main risks: confusing registration date with income timing; missing zero weeks in a sparse series; treating unknown values as zero; losing sign below baseline; inaccessible SVG with a hover-only value; header overflow for long names or large totals; and accidentally changing currency behavior for funder details. Existing tests assert `.member-total`, `.signed-bar`, and count text, so update affected assertions rather than preserving summary scaffolding. No files in the repository were changed.

## Candidate rationale

This candidate keeps data semantics and exact decimal arithmetic in the existing typed entity module and keeps SVG rendering as a narrow presentation component. An all-CSS graph would reuse current bars but makes time-axis labels, negative values, sparse buckets, and screen-reader descriptions awkward. A general chart package would add dependency surface for two simple charts. Inlining all aggregation in Svelte would shorten file count but mix money/date domain rules into markup and make edge cases harder to reason about.

